{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 29, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a prominent figure in the Crusades and a powerful ruler of the County of Tripoli during the 12th century. He served as Count from 1155 until his death in 1193. His reign marked a period of significant expansion and consolidation of Tripoli's territory and influence in the Levant.\n\n*Early Life and Ascension* Raymond III was born in 1126 to Raymond I Count of Tripoli and his wife Adela of England. He received a thorough education in military strategy diplomacy and law. He inherited the County of Tripoli upon his father's death in 1155.  Immediately he faced challenges from neighboring powers including the Byzantine Empire and the Seljuk Turks. He skillfully navigated these complex political landscapes establishing alliances and maintaining a strong defensive position. His early years were characterized by military campaigns and internal reforms aimed at strengthening the county's administration.\n\nRaymond III’s reign witnessed the County of Tripoli's growing importance as a major power in the Levant. He actively participated in the Third Crusade alongside King Louis VII and other European knights.  He played a crucial role in securing provisions and support for the Crusade.  His military prowess and diplomatic skills were widely recognized enhancing Tripoli's prestige.  He also expanded the county's territory through strategic alliances and military victories consolidating control over key coastal regions and trade routes.\n\n*Political and Military Achievements*  Raymond III’s political acumen was a key factor in his success. He fostered strong relationships with various Christian and Muslim powers. He skillfully balanced competing interests ensuring Tripoli's security and prosperity. He reformed the county's legal system and promoted economic growth through trade and agriculture.  His military campaigns were focused on 